    "description": "This long-term project is a tribute to all those, like Donna, who have left everything behind to build a better future for their families. Through her journey, I explore the human cost of exile, but also the resilience it reveals—particularly the invisible threads that bind loved ones across borders. In exile, technology has become a vital lifeline. It allows Donna to remain a mother, a daughter, and a friend—despite the physical separation.\n\nDonna, 47, comes from a remote mountain village in the Philippines. The eldest daughter of farmers, she dreamt of becoming a nurse but left her country to pay for her children’s education. Before arriving in France 13 years ago, she paid a smuggler €13,000. Since then, she has worked full-time for a wealthy Libyan family, sending every euro home. Her sacrifices bore fruit: her eldest daughter, Nicole, is now a qualified nurse. Yet, Donna has not seen her children or parents in over a decade.\n\nHer connection to them survives thanks to smartphones, patchy Wi-Fi, and a routine of voice messages and video calls. Technology allows her to witness birthdays, send lullabies, and offer comfort after a bad school day. These moments—fleeting but essential—are the only form of presence she can offer. I have documented this intimacy from both sides: in France, where Donna works and lives in the shadows, and in the Philippines, where her voice echoes in the family home through a cracked phone screen.\n\nThe story also speaks to the broader experience of nearly 10 million overseas Filipino workers, 80% of whom are women. In France alone, nearly 50,000 Filipinas work as domestic helpers, many of them undocumented. They are part of a global chain of care that remains largely unseen, yet sustains entire economies and generations.\n\nThrough Donna’s story, I illustrate what sociologist Abdelmalek Sayad called the “double absence”—immigrants are both invisible here and missed there. But technology has carved out a third space: one of digital presence, where love, guidance, and identity still circulate. In this space, exile is not just defined by separation, but by connection.",
